For a complete binary tree, compare the height of the leftmost path with the height of the rightmost path. If they match, the current subtree is perfect, so its count is `2^height - 1`.

If they do not match, one side is still perfect and the remaining work is pushed into the incomplete side. The formula uses a shift instead of floating point math.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public int countNodes(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        int leftHeight = getLeftHeight(root);
        int rightHeight = getRightHeight(root);

        if (leftHeight == rightHeight) {
            return (1 << leftHeight) - 1;
        }

        return 1 + countNodes(root.left) + countNodes(root.right);
    }

    private int getLeftHeight(TreeNode node) {
        int height = 0;
        while (node != null) {
            height++;
            node = node.left;
        }
        return height;
    }

    private int getRightHeight(TreeNode node) {
        int height = 0;
        while (node != null) {
            height++;
            node = node.right;
        }
        return height;
    }
}
```

Dry run on a perfect tree with seven nodes: both paths have height 3, so the answer is `(1 << 3) - 1`, which is 7. No child subtree needs to be visited.

The empty tree returns 0. For a complete tree that is not perfect, the recursive calls continue only where the missing nodes can be.

Time complexity: `O(log^2 n)`, because height checks take `O(log n)` and there can be `O(log n)` levels of recursion.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` from the recursion stack.

This is the direct counting version. Each call returns the number of nodes below that pointer. A null pointer contributes zero, while a real node contributes one.

It is not the faster complete-tree method, but the code is short and easy to reuse when the tree may not actually be complete.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    int countNodes(TreeNode* root) {
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return 0;
        }

        int leftCount = countNodes(root->left);
        int rightCount = countNodes(root->right);
        return 1 + leftCount + rightCount;
    }
};
```

Dry run for a root with two leaf children:

```text
count(root) = 1 + count(left) + count(right)
             = 1 + 1 + 1
             = 3
```

A null root returns zero, and a one-node tree returns one.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since all nodes are processed.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the call stack, with `h` as the tree height.

A queue gives a simple level order traversal. Start with the root, remove one node at a time, increase the count, and add its children to the queue.

The complete-tree property is not needed for this version. That makes it a useful basic check when the main goal is just to count every node safely.

```csharp
/*
 // Definition for a binary tree node.
 public class TreeNode {
     public int val;
     public TreeNode left;
     public TreeNode right;

     public TreeNode(int val = 0, TreeNode left = null, TreeNode right = null) {
         this.val = val;
         this.left = left;
         this.right = right;
     }
 }
*/

using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int CountNodes(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new Queue<TreeNode>();
        queue.Enqueue(root);
        int count = 0;

        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            TreeNode current = queue.Dequeue();
            count++;

            if (current.left != null) {
                queue.Enqueue(current.left);
            }

            if (current.right != null) {
                queue.Enqueue(current.right);
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

For the tree `1 -> 2, 3`, the queue changes like this: `[1]`, then `[2, 3]`, then `[3]`, and finally `[]`. The counter ends at 3.

The queue can contain many nodes on a wide level, so this uses more memory than the recursive version on some trees.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(w)`, where `w` is the maximum number of nodes on one level.

The empty tree is the only special case here. For a normal recursive count, add the counts from the left and right children, then add one for the current node. This also works for any binary tree, so it does not depend on the tree being complete.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public int countNodes(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        return 1 + countNodes(root.left) + countNodes(root.right);
    }
}
```

For example, a tree with root 1 and children 2 and 3 gives `1 + 1 + 1 = 3`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, because every node is visited once.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursive calls, where `h` is the tree height.

